program Bug_2_17_KrillsLoaderCaseSensitiveValidation;
// Bug 2.17: Parser::HandleKrillsLoader (parser.cpp ~6521) parses the
// three address tokens normally, then reconstructs its own idea of the
// canonical source text ("@use KrillsLoader " + hex + " " + hex + " " +
// hex) and checks whether that EXACT string literally appears in
// m_lexer->m_text - the only real validation the directive does. Every
// other directive in the language matches its own keyword
// case-insensitively (via .toLower()), but this reconstructed string uses
// the fixed casing "KrillsLoader", so writing the keyword in any other
// casing (lowercase "krillsloader" here) fails validation even though the
// three parsed address values are entirely valid and identical to a
// working line (docs-examples/initkrill.ras uses the exact same three
// addresses with the documented "KrillsLoader" casing and compiles fine).
//
// Method B (compile-time), no VICE step. Confirmed live: fails with
// "Something went wrong with the krill loader implementation: please make
// sure that the loader line is exactly of the following format (including
// spaces and letter cases etc): '@use KrillsLoader $0200 $2000 $3000 '".
// Post-fix (validating the parsed integer values directly instead of
// re-deriving and string-matching the source line) this should compile
// clean, same as the "KrillsLoader" casing already does today.
@use krillsloader $0200 $2000 $3000

begin
	InitKrill();
	loop();
end.
